// runtime/stdlib/hash.kara
//
// CR-202 slice 5e. Per design.md § Hash — Hash is parameterized over
// the hasher type so that `Map[K, V, FxBuildHasher]` and
// `Map[K, V, SipHashBuildHasher]` can share key impls. The actual
// `Hasher` trait is not yet baked, so this declaration omits the
// `H: Hasher` bound — the bound resurfaces when slice 5+ migrates the
// `Hasher` trait alongside the rest of the hashing infrastructure.
//
// Pre-5e Hash was not registered in `env.traits` at all
// (register_stdlib_traits's array doesn't list it). Slice 5e is
// strictly additive.

trait Hash {
    fn hash[H](ref self, hasher: mut ref H);
}

// ── The two hasher selectors ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// design.md § `Hash` and `Hasher`, "Default hasher for v1", spells both:
// "`Map[K, V]` and `Set[T]` without an explicit hasher use
// `SipHash13BuildHasher`, seeded from a per-process random source ... Users
// who pin the hasher (`Map[K, V, FxBuildHasher]`) opt out of both the
// DoS-resistance guarantee and the version-stability escape hatch."
//
// WHAT THESE ARE TODAY: type-level SELECTORS, not user-implementable hashers.
// The trailing type argument of `Map[K, V, H]` / `Set[T, H]` must name one of
// exactly these two; the typechecker rejects anything else, and the choice
// picks which hash the two backends emit. `trait BuildHasher` and the
// `Hasher` trait design.md also declares are NOT baked, so a user cannot yet
// write their own hasher and name it here — that is a separate, larger
// feature (an ordinary trait with default method bodies, dispatched per
// hash), tracked on its own ledger row rather than half-shipped here.
//
// They are declared as zero-field structs because that is what they will be
// when `BuildHasher` does land: `impl BuildHasher for FxBuildHasher` needs a
// nominal type to hang off, and a config-free hasher holds no state. Nothing
// constructs one today.

// SipHash-1-3 with a per-process random key — the DEFAULT for every `Map` and
// `Set` that does not name a hasher, and the reason iteration order differs
// from one process to the next. Naming it explicitly is exactly equivalent to
// leaving the parameter off; it exists so that intent can be written down.
struct SipHash13BuildHasher { }

// FxHash — rotate-xor-multiply against a fixed multiplier, no key. Faster on
// short keys, and floodable by anyone who can read the multiplier out of the
// compiler's source. Because it is unkeyed, iteration order is stable across
// runs of one binary. Reach for it only when the keys cannot come from an
// adversary, and never for `Map[String, _]` keyed on request data.
struct FxBuildHasher { }
